left.\n- 6 at (6,5) is 4 from the bottom and 6 from the top, so it goes down. It also goes left.\n- 5 at (7,11) is 4 from the right edge, so it goes right. It also goes down.\n\nNone of these rays cross each other.\n\n**Construction:** I start from a copy of the input. For each seed in turn, I paint its vertical ray and then its horizontal ray, and emit the canvas after each seed's L is drawn.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    seeds = [(y, x, int(g[y, x])) for y in range(H) for x in range(W) if g[y, x] != bg]\n    for y, x, c in seeds:\n        # vertical ray toward the nearest horizontal edge\n        if y <= H - 1 - y:\n            out[0:y, x] = c\n            vdir = \"up\"\n        else:\n            out[y + 1:H, x] = c\n            vdir = \"down\"\n        # horizontal ray toward the nearest vertical edge\n        if x <= W - 1 - x:\n            out[y, 0:x] = c\n            hdir = \"left\"\n        else:\n            out[y, x + 1:W] = c\n            hdir = \"right\"\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Seed colour {c} at ({y},{x}): ray {vdir} and ray {hdir} to the nearest edges.\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"All seeds have drawn their L-shaped rays; final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In the output, each cell sends out two straight rays in its own colour, forming an L."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"One ray is vertical and one is horizontal, and both run to the grid border."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The position of the cell does decide it: each cell shoots toward its **nearest horizontal edge** and its **nearest vertical edge**."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"I measure the distance to an edge as y versus H−1−y, and x versus W−1−x."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"d1618ff9e7e61851a913d2b9660e29c9a75e169dc